<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle March (al-Andalus)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marca Media</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle March was the central frontier district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> facing the Christian kingdoms of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, with its capital first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and from 946 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinaceli">Medinaceli</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> it was held by military governors and frontier lords. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> collapsed in the early 11th century, the march broke up into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> such as Toledo and Molina.</p></div>
